{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Yitzhak loved, etc.</b> Now, according to the words of the Midrash Tanchuma of blessed memory, which says that he would trap his father with words — how to tithe salt — he was not particular to do so except for Yitzhak and not for Rivka, because his concern was not for the sake of the mitzvah but rather because he feared his curse, and also for the hope of blessings — which was not the case with Rivka. And therefore you will find that he did not say ״יִקְרְבוּ יְמֵי אֵבֶל אָבִי וְאִמִּי״ but only ״אָבִי.״ And the reason Rivka did not tell Yitzhak of his wickedness — perhaps he would deceive him, not in her presence, to conceal from her the cause of his love for him, so as to remove it. And according to Onkelos, who translated ״אֲרֵי מִצֵּידֵיהּ הֲוָה אָכֵיל״ — one needs to know why this reason does not also avail for Rivka, for by simple logic Yitzhak would not have exerted himself more than Rivka [did]. And perhaps it is because Rivka — her maintenance is incumbent upon Yitzhak to feed her meat, as the Maharik wrote in Even HaEzer (sec. 70, clause 1), that he is obligated in her food, and if the members of her family are accustomed to luxuries he must conduct himself accordingly — end quote. And it is known that Betuel was a prince and grandee of Aram; therefore she would not consider it a favor to have game in her mouth."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The phrase כִּי לֹא לְצַד הַמִּצְוָה appears to refer back to Esav's behavior of tithe-trapping; the subject throughout is Esav and his deceptive displays of piety directed only at Yitzhak."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:43:23.240322288Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}